WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.410
LORIN PRICE: My
name is Lorin Price.

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.840
I'm a Networking Specialist
Customer Engineer

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.860
at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.380
And today I'm going to talk
to you about the networking

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.600
concepts for Cloud
Data Fusion that

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.570
are helpful to know for
implementation, security,

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:14.680
and connectivity.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.380
[INTRO MUSIC]

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.130
Cloud Data Fusion is
Google Cloud's native

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.180
managed service for enterprise
data integration and ingestion.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.790
It provides a visual
point-and-click interface

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:32.770
enabling a code free deployment
of ETL and ELT data pipelines.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:36.250
Cloud Data Fusion has a number
of components that exist behind

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.380
the scenes that
power the service,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.140
and it creates a
tenant project and VPC

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.360
to hold those resources.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.570
The Data Fusion components
that are surfaced to the user

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.260
are the Data Fusion instance
and the Dataproc clusters.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.980
Each Cloud Data Fusion instance
contains a unique independent

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.170
Cloud Data Fusion
deployment that

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.230
contains a set of services
that handle pipeline lifecycle

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.380
management, orchestration,
coordination,

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.360
and metadata management.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.420
Cloud Data Fusion creates
ephemeral execution

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.450
environments to run
pipelines when you manually

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.700
run your pipelines
or when pipelines

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.720
run through a time schedule
or a pipeline state trigger.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.800
Cloud Data Fusion uses Dataproc
as an execution environment

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.340
in which you can choose to run
pipelines as Spark or Spark

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.200
streaming programs.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.650
Cloud Data Fusion provisions
an ephemeral Dataproc cluster

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.530
in the user project at the
beginning of a pipeline

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.520
run, executes the pipeline
using Map Reduce or Spark

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.090
in the cluster, and then deletes
the cluster after the pipeline

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.070
execution is complete.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.130
Alternatively, if you manage
your Dataproc clusters

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.260
in controlled
environments, you can also

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.070
configure Cloud Data Fusion not
to provision ephemeral clusters

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.840
and run pipelines against
existing data clusters instead.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.520
The Cloud Data
Fusion instance can

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.350
be thought of as the
design environment

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.130
and the Dataproc cluster as
the execution environment.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.470
There are two types of
Cloud Data Fusion instances

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.410
based on an access
model, a public instance,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.840
which is the default deployment,
and a private instance.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.540
Let's dig into the
access patterns

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.580
from different scenarios.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.060
We can start with the public
IP deployment pattern.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.760
In a public instance,
network communication

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.250
happens over the open
internet regardless

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.860
of the source location,
which is not recommended

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.750
for critical environments.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.570
The Cloud Data Fusion instance
for the design environment

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.200
will have a public IP.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.460
However, the data cluster used
for the execution environment

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.420
can be deployed with either
public IP or private IPs.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.080
Let's move on to the private
IP deployment pattern.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.260
Cloud Data Fusion private
IP has different setup steps

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.780
depending on whether you
are using a standard VPC

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.120
or a shared RPC network.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.250
If you're using a
standard VPC, you

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.440
can create the instance
using either the Cloud

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.550
Console or a cURL command.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.000
However, if you are
using a shared VPC,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.890
you must use a cURL command.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.450
Check out the
documentation that's

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.730
linked in the description
for more information.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.220
When private IP
instances are created,

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.140
a separate tenant
project is created

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.780
for each user project in
which Cloud Data Fusion

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.910
instances are provisioned.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.850
Additionally, Cloud
Data Fusion private IP

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.880
requires peering in connection
to communicate privately

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.400
with sources located
in the user VPC

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.670
and is required for the design
environment capabilities.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.090
This peering connection
must be created manually.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.690
If you need to access a public
source using a private IP Cloud

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.300
Data Fusion instance
when designing pipelines,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.750
you must use version
6.4 or above.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.540
In the design environment,
Cloud Data Fusion traffic

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.670
will traverse a VPC peer from
the tenon VPC to the user VPC.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.950
Traffic will then be
directed to an internal load

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:55.720
balancer fronting a managed
instance group of gateway VMs.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.120
Traffic will egress
through the gateway VMs

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.460
through a Cloud NAT
configured for the subnet

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.040
into the public source
on the internet.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.980
The Dataproc cluster used
for the execution environment

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.170
should be configured to
egress through the Cloud NAT

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.690
as well to reach
the public source.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.540
For sources that are
hosted within the user VPC,

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.810
Cloud Data Fusion will
connect over a VPC peer.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.630
Firewalls must be
configured in the source VPC

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.480
to allow the source to
communicate with the Cloud Data

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.040
Fusion instance, as well as the
Dataproc execution environment.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.770
If you are in a peered hub
and spoke architecture model

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.470
and you are trying to make
a connection from a VPC that

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:36.220
is not directly peered to the
Cloud Data Fusion tenant VPC,

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.190
you must use a proxy
due to the nontransitive

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.860
routing nature of Google's VPCs.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:45.010
This will only be needed
for the design environment.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.320
Be aware you can also
replace a peering hub

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.300
and spoke architecture
with a VPN hub

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.480
and spoke model so that the
Cloud Data Fusion 10 and BPC

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.960
route can be advertised
over the VPN.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.510
Nontransitive peering
also comes into play

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.110
when trying to connect to
a Google Cloud source that

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.450
is deployed using
another tenant project,

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.980
for example, Cloud SQL, Cloud
Data Fusion and Cloud SQL

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.020
are both hosted in
tenant projects,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.760
but these projects
are different.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.880
In order for Cloud Data Fusion
to connect to a Cloud SQL

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.550
source, a proxy VM
must be deployed

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.600
in the user VPC to proxy traffic
to the Cloud SQL instance

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.060
when using the
design environment.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.330
Since the Dataproc clusters used
in the execution environment

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.700
are hosted in the
user VPC, they will

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.080
be able to connect to Cloud SQL
without the use of the proxy.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.170
When accessing sources in an
on prem environment with Cloud

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.930
Data Fusion private
IP, a hybrid connection

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:44.130
must exist between the user
VPC and on prem environment,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.700
either a HA VPN or
interconnect can be used.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.640
Both the IP side arrange
for the tenant VPC

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.910
where the Cloud Data Fusion
instance is hosted as well

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.730
as the IP side range
of the Dataproc cluster

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.580
in the user VPC
must be advertised

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:00.900
over the hybrid connection.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.530
And on prem firewalls must be
configured to accept ingress

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.540
from these ranges.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.290
If the VLAN or VPN lands
in another peered VPC, then

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.320
you'll need to use a proxy to
connect the source to the Cloud

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.690
Data Fusion instance.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.690
When accessing data sources,
both public and private

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.230
instances make outgoing
calls to Google Cloud APIs

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.210
using private Google access.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.750
Let's briefly cover
some security topics.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.810
For Cloud Data Fusion
versions below 6.5,

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:34.140
you can only manage access
at an instance level.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.950
If you have access
to an instance,

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.230
you have access to all pipelines
and metadata in that instance.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.960
For version 6.5
and above, you also

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.750
have the ability to use
role-based access control

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:49.200
to establish name space level
isolation within a single Cloud

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.700
Data Fusion instance.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.520
This feature is
currently in preview.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.550
Pipeline access to data is
provided by granting access

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.460
to the service account,
which can be a custom service

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.200
account that you specify.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.170
You must make sure that the
service account has proper IAM

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:07.320
permissions in place to access
any Google Cloud hosted data

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.730
sources.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.150
For a pipeline execution, you
control ingress and egress

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.890
by setting the
appropriate firewall

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:20.140
rules on the user VPC on which
the pipeline is being executed.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.570
Before we wrap up, let's
summarize the three most

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.700
important takeaways from today.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.640
Cloud Data Fusion exists
as a design environment

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.160
that runs within a
tenant project in VPC

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.550
and an execution environment
that runs as a data cluster

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.590
within the user VPC.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:37.420
Cloud Data Fusion
can be launched

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:41.920
with a public IP or private IP,
and VPC peering must manually

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.590
be configured between
the tenant VPC and user

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:47.140
VPC for private IP.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.230
Firewall rules must be
configured to allow connections

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.740
to and from both the Cloud
Data Fusion instant [? sider ?]

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.860
and the Dataproc [? sider. ?]
Thanks for watching,

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:59.890
and look out for another video
on the concepts of networking

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.780
for Google Cloud's
managed services.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

